      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T22:26:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done",
      "parentUuid": "f29a4c18_bafb9a52",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "047980e1_4b180f65",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-13T21:50:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "elements are allocated once and never deleted? Are we guaranteed that all initializations occur before any accesses? Are the elements static once init is done?",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9258cf9c_739f7ffc",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 37,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T00:58:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, the pointers are static once init is done. Before initialization they\u0027re NULL, and all accesses have null checks before dereferencing.",
      "parentUuid": "047980e1_4b180f65",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3b45d88a_7905584b",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-13T21:50:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Can next_offset be modified while we are reading it or is it static after init is complete?",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fcd8be89_a0577871",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T00:58:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Both, because tasks can be initialized and call this code before init is complete.",
      "parentUuid": "3b45d88a_7905584b",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fcabd7f6_a22a3c8f",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T01:38:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What if that happens... it does\u0027t matter which version of next_offset (old or new) is used to initialize p-\u003emax_state?",
      "parentUuid": "fcd8be89_a0577871",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3758ac8b_982cc771",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T02:03:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding is that the compiler could see that next_offset is not modified within this function and do something like\n\ntemp \u003d kcalloc(next_offset, ...);\n...\np-\u003emax_state \u003d next_offset;\n\nwhich could race with next_offset being incremented in cpufreq_times_create_policy. I guess READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE would be enough to prevent this though. And the atomic_add_return() during policy creation is unnecessary given Saravana\u0027s comment that policy creation can\u0027t race.",
      "parentUuid": "fcabd7f6_a22a3c8f",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e320de5_6757e32b",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T19:43:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My main question is the race for max_state (not its consistency). If there is a concurrent thread in create_policy, next_offset can be modified while we are here, so max_state might get the old version (before the other thread modifies next_offset) or it might get the new version (after the the other thread modifies next_offset).\n\nIf we are using the old value, is there a chance that we allocated too little memory (memory allocated and then max_state is increased). Might we overflow the array later when we index by a state that we didn\u0027t know about at allocation time?\n\nUnless we reallocate time_in_state for all tasks that were previously initialized, it seems like we need to know the max before allocation and it needs to be stable and especially never increase.",
      "parentUuid": "3758ac8b_982cc771",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "50f008fb_87e71bfd",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T19:54:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Both times we index into p-\u003etime_in_state, we first check whether the indices are less than p-\u003emax_state (line 95, line 123). This means tasks will track time_in_state data only for policies initialized before they started, but they will never overflow the array.\n\nWe could still reallocate for the sake of more comprehensive data (although the existing pixel patches don\u0027t, so it might be expensive), but it\u0027s not necessary to avoid overflows.",
      "parentUuid": "3e320de5_6757e32b",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "418523ed_c02e1150",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T20:20:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Oh. Doesn\u0027t this mean that if a big cluster is offline (eg, cpus 2-3 on pixel 1) when a process starts, it will never get stats for the big cluster even when the cpus come online?\n\nI think policies are freed if all the cpus in a cluster are offlined (correct?). If so, when they come back online are they considered \"new\" and therefore no stats for any task that was started earlier?\n\nWhat are the cases that trigger creation of a new policy?",
      "parentUuid": "50f008fb_87e71bfd",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "160e2cb8_8d8a2801",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T20:36:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "policy was the wrong choice of words on my part. If a cluster has ever had a policy initialized, even if it is later offlined and its policy is freed, its frequency table is permanently stored in all_freqs and tasks initialized after that point will have data for it. I don\u0027t think we need to do anything about subsequent policy destruction or creation for that cluster since the frequency table stays the same.",
      "parentUuid": "418523ed_c02e1150",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8a249627_397afe70",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T22:00:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I see us guarding against re-initializing all_freqs() so we are just left with the case of tasks that start before a cluster has ever been brought online. This is probably not an interesting case for android, but it could be an important case for upstream if we plan to send this upstream (online addition of numa nodes etc). Are we still planning to submit this upstream? The global lock could also become an issue in that case.",
      "parentUuid": "160e2cb8_8d8a2801",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "52afed26_2ed7f789",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_times.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 52,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115183
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-14T22:26:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I had assumed we still wanted to send this patch upstream if possible. Should I go ahead and try adding code to reallocate p-\u003etime_in_state? I think it could just be a function called from acct_update_power, either when p-\u003emax_state \u003c next_offset or when p-\u003emax_state \u003c state\n\nWould the alternative to the global lock be per-task locks? I guess another option would be to protect the pointer to p-\u003etime_in_state using RCU since most accesses only modify its entries, not the pointer itself.",
      "parentUuid": "8a249627_397afe70",
      "revId": "6f13fe4149cf156862dcdbe83078b07fd70895c8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
